window.COLLEGES = [{"id":1,"name":"All Souls College","slug":"all-souls-college","body":"If Oxford had a secret society cloaked in plain daylight, All Souls would be it. Founded back in 1438 by King Henry VI and Archbishop Henry Chichele to pray for the souls of fallen soldiers from the Hundred Years' War, this college does not take undergraduate applicants. In fact, it is purely a fellowship of elite academics, world-renowned researchers, and former UK prime ministers who survived one of the most notoriously terrifying entrance exams on Earth.\n\nStepping past its imposing front gates feels like crossing into an alternate timeline where quiet contemplation is an Olympic sport. The Great Quad, framed by Nicholas Hawksmoor’s dramatic twin Gothic towers and Christopher Wren’s legendary sundial, radiates an aura of quiet power. The college library, with its soaring ceilings and miles of scholarly treatises, offers an atmosphere so serene you can practically hear the centuries ticking away.\n\nYet behind all that prestige lies one of Oxford’s most eccentric traditions: the Mallard Ceremony. Once every century, the Fellows march through the college quads late at night, carrying torches, singing a ballad about a mythical giant duck that supposedly flew out of the foundations when the college was built, and chasing a wooden duck on a pole. It is the perfect reminder that even the sharpest minds in academia love a little absurd theater.","image":"assets/colleges/all-souls-college.jpg","lat":51.7534,"lng":-1.2531,"founded":1438,"hook":"Fellows only. No students at all."},{"id":2,"name":"Balliol College","slug":"balliol-college","body":"Balliol likes to proudly introduce itself as the intellectual powerhouse that basically runs the world—or at least British politics. Founded in 1263 after John de Balliol was publicly whipped for insulting the Bishop of Durham and ordered to pay penance by funding poor scholars, Balliol grew into a fierce bastion of debate, philosophy, and political activism. If you are looking for fiery discussions over breakfast about ethics and statecraft, you will find them here.\n\nThe atmosphere around Balliol's front quad is warm, lively, and unapologetically progressive. Its student common room was among the first to welcome radical student movements, and its alumni roster reads like a Who’s Who of world leaders, from prime ministers like Boris Johnson and Harold Macmillan to legendary economists like Adam Smith. It is the kind of place where every hallway conversation feels like prep for a future cabinet meeting or a revolutionary manifesto.\n\nDespite the heavy political pedigree, Balliol students know how to let their hair down and engage in legendary neighborhood drama. Situated right next door to Trinity College, Balliol maintains one of Oxford’s oldest and most playful feuds, exchanging satirical chants and lawn pranks for centuries. When they are not trading witty barbs over the garden wall, you will find students lounging on the lawns with their beloved tortoises during the annual intercollegiate turtle races.","image":"assets/colleges/balliol-college.jpg","lat":51.7548,"lng":-1.2578,"founded":1263,"hook":"Britain's prime-minister factory."},{"id":3,"name":"Brasenose College","slug":"brasenose-college","body":"Nestled right in the beating heart of Oxford overlooking the iconic Radcliffe Camera, Brasenose College—affectionately known as BNC—radiates charisma, athletic energy, and good humor. Founded in 1509, its curious name comes from a quirky 12th-century brass door knocker shaped like a roaring beast's nose (\"brazen nose\"). In medieval times, students actually stole this talisman away to Stamford during a university dispute, only for the college to triumphantly buy it back centuries later.\n\nWalking into Brasenose, you immediately sense a balance between ancient dignity and approachable charm. The college's golden honey-colored stone walls catch the afternoon sunlight, creating one of the coziest, most photogenic dining and social environments in the university. Students gather in the buttery or the lively common room, carrying on a tradition of being one of the friendliest and most well-rounded colleges on campus.\n\nBeyond the cozy quads and historic brass talismans, Brasenose boasts an incredible history of producing visionary leaders, notable authors like William Golding, and sporting legends. On Ale Verse Day, an ancient tradition celebrated on Ascension Day, students compose satirical rhymes about college gossip in exchange for free beer flavored with ivy leaves. It is this mix of warm camaraderie and playful heritage that makes BNC an unforgettable stop.","image":"assets/colleges/brasenose-college.jpg","lat":51.753,"lng":-1.2549,"founded":1509,"hook":"Named after a brass door knocker."},{"id":4,"name":"Christ Church","slug":"christ-church","body":"Christ Church is the blockbuster giant of Oxford, towering with sheer grandiosity, royal drama, and cinematic fame. Founded in 1525 by Cardinal Wolsey (and later taken over by King Henry VIII after Wolsey fell from royal grace), Christ Church is so massive it even houses its own full-scale cathedral within its walls. From the iconic Tom Tower designed by Sir Christopher Wren to its sprawling meadow, Christ Church feels less like a quiet school and more like an independent city-state.\n\nEvery day, visitors flock to admire its breathtaking Great Hall, whose hammerbeam wooden roof, grand portraits, and long dining tables served as the direct inspiration for the Hogwarts dining hall in the *Harry Potter* film franchise. But beyond the cinematic stardom, Christ Church was the creative nursery where mathematics don Charles Dodgson (pen name Lewis Carroll) spun whimsical tales about a girl named Alice, inspired by the Dean’s daughter, Alice Liddell.\n\nYet beneath all its royal pomp, Christ Church operates on its own delightfully stubborn sense of time. To this day, Great Tom—the six-ton bell in the central tower—rings 101 times every night at 9:05 PM (which corresponds to 9:00 PM Oxford local solar time, five minutes behind Greenwich Mean Time), signaling the ancient curfew for the original 101 scholars. It is a daily acoustic reminder that tradition here never bows to modern convenience.","image":"assets/colleges/christ-church.jpg","lat":51.7503,"lng":-1.255,"founded":1546,"hook":"Its hall became Hogwarts."},{"id":5,"name":"Corpus Christi College","slug":"corpus-christi-college","body":"Corpus Christi might be one of Oxford’s smallest colleges by land area, but what it lacks in square footage it makes up for in pure heart, intellectual grit, and fierce pride. Founded in 1517 by Richard Foxe, Bishop of Winchester, Corpus was built from day one as a beacon for Renaissance Humanism, with a library so rich in classical Greek and Latin manuscripts that the scholar Erasmus famously praised it as a wonder of the academic world.\n\nAt the center of its intimate main quad stands the famous Pelican Sundial—a magnificent 16th-century column topped with a golden pelican pecking its breast to feed its young, surrounded by an intricate multi-faceted solar timepiece. Because the college is so compact, the community feels tight-knit and familial; everyone knows your name, and you cannot walk across the quad without running into a classmate eager to trade book recommendations.\n\nCorpus is also famous across Oxford for hosting the legendary Annual Tortoise Fair. Every May, college tortoises from across Oxford descend upon Corpus's gardens to race toward a circle of fresh lettuce leaves, cheered on by hundreds of students sipping Pimm's. Their own resident tortoise, Foxe, is treated like royalty, serving as the calm, shelled mascot of an endlessly charming academic sanctuary.","image":"assets/colleges/corpus-christi-college.jpg","lat":51.7511,"lng":-1.2545,"founded":1517,"hook":"Hosts the tortoise races."},{"id":6,"name":"Exeter College","slug":"exeter-college","body":"Founded in 1314 by Walter de Stapledon, Bishop of Exeter, this is the fourth-oldest college in Oxford, steeped in a timeless Devonshire spirit. Tucked neatly along Turl Street, Exeter opens up like a hidden treasure chest of stunning architecture, featuring a breathtaking neo-Gothic chapel modeled closely after the Sainte-Chapelle in Paris and an idyllic, elevated Fellows’ Garden overlooking the iconic dome of the Radcliffe Camera.\n\nExeter’s literary legacy is the stuff of absolute fantasy lore. J.R.R. Tolkien walked these cobblestone paths as an undergraduate studying Old Norse and Germanic linguistics, absorbing the mythic atmosphere that would later inspire the halls of Middle-earth. Philip Pullman also studied here, later immortalizing the college as \"Jordan College\" in his *His Dark Materials* trilogy, complete with secret rooftop scrambles and daemons lounging in the quads.\n\nLife at Exeter strikes a harmonious chord between creative imagination and warm student tradition. The college bar is famously tucked into a medieval cellar, buzzing with spirited discussions, while the choir fills the ornate Victorian chapel with world-class choral harmonies every week. Standing in the Fellows’ Garden at twilight as the Oxford bells ring across the skyline is an experience that feels straight out of a storybook.","image":"assets/colleges/exeter-college.jpg","lat":51.7541,"lng":-1.2559,"founded":1314,"hook":"Tolkien's college."},{"id":7,"name":"Green Templeton College","slug":"green-templeton-college","body":"Green Templeton College (GTC) represents the vibrant, forward-looking modern chapter of Oxford’s collegiate journey. Born in 2008 from the merger of Green College (founded in 1979 by medical pioneer Sir Cecil Green) and Templeton College (founded in 1965 by business visionary Sir John Templeton), this graduate-only college is dedicated entirely to contemporary global challenges, bringing together bright minds in medicine, management, education, and social sciences.\n\nThe visual centerpiece of GTC is the magnificent Radcliffe Observatory, an 18th-century architectural masterpiece inspired by Athens' ancient Tower of the Winds. Topped by a gleaming sculpture of Atlas and Hercules holding up the celestial sphere, the observatory grounds provide an airy, tranquil oasis in the lively bohemian neighborhood of Jericho. Graduate researchers gather inside its historic observation rooms to share groundbreaking insights over coffee.\n\nUnlike ancient colleges with rigid medieval formalisms, Green Templeton champions an egalitarian, collaborative, and inclusive culture. There is no traditional \"High Table\" separating professors from students in the dining hall; world-renowned epidemiologists, MBA candidates, and clinical doctors eat side-by-side. It is a refreshing, globally minded community where the focus is firmly on solving the real-world problems of tomorrow.","image":null,"lat":51.7614,"lng":-1.265,"founded":2008,"hook":"Built round an observatory."},{"id":8,"name":"Harris Manchester College","slug":"harris-manchester-college","body":"Harris Manchester College holds a uniquely empowering mission in Oxford: it is dedicated exclusively to students aged 21 and older who are returning to higher education. Originally established in Warrington in 1786 as an academy for nonconformist religious thinkers and reformers, the institution moved to Oxford in 1889 and attained full collegiate status in 1996. Its motto, \"Truth, Liberty, Religion,\" reflects a centuries-old dedication to intellectual freedom and second chances.\n\nTucked quietly behind Mansfield Road, the college is a visual gem boasting a chapel adorned with breathtaking stained glass windows designed by Pre-Raphaelite masters Edward Burne-Jones and William Morris. The tranquil Tate Library, with its polished oak desks and clocktower views, provides mature students with one of the most peaceful, supportive environments in the entire university to reignite their academic passions.\n\nThe student body here brings an extraordinary wealth of life experience, from former military veterans and corporate professionals to artists, parents, and self-taught innovators. The dining hall buzzes with life stories that stretch far beyond conventional school pathways, creating an unpretentious, supportive, and deeply inspiring academic family. At Harris Manchester, age is celebrated as your greatest intellectual superpower.","image":"assets/colleges/harris-manchester-college.jpg","lat":51.7556,"lng":-1.2532,"founded":1786,"hook":"Mature students only."},{"id":9,"name":"Hertford College","slug":"hertford-college","body":"If you have ever seen a postcard of Oxford featuring the romantic \"Bridge of Sighs\" arching gracefully over New College Lane, you have already caught a glimpse of Hertford College. Re-founded in its modern form in 1874 (with origins reaching back to Hart Hall in the 1280s), Hertford famously connects its two main campus quads via this striking 20th-century skybridge, giving students a daily skywalk between library sessions and dorm rooms.\n\nHertford is celebrated throughout Oxford as a trailblazing pioneer of progressive access and social inclusivity. In the 1960s, it led the famous \"Hertford Scheme,\" actively reaching out to state schools and breaking down historical socioeconomic barriers to an Oxford education. It was also among the very first all-male colleges to open its doors to female students in 1974, cementing its reputation as an open-minded, down-to-earth community.\n\nCulturally, Hertford has a mischievous, literary soul—it was the college of Evelyn Waugh, who immortalized its decadent, eccentric personalities in his masterpiece *Brideshead Revisited*. Today, the college balances that witty, creative heritage with a passionate sporting culture and a beloved social hub in its subterranean student bar. Walking across the Bridge of Sighs on a foggy Oxford morning remains an essential rite of passage.","image":"assets/colleges/hertford-college.jpg","lat":51.7541,"lng":-1.254,"founded":1282,"hook":"The Bridge of Sighs."},{"id":10,"name":"Jesus College","slug":"jesus-college","body":"Proudly founded in 1571 during the reign of Queen Elizabeth I, Jesus College has long carried the affectionate title of \"The Welsh College\" of Oxford. Established upon the petition of Welsh clergyman Hugh Price, the college maintained deep historic ties with Wales for centuries, endowing generations of Welsh scholars, poets, and thinkers with an intellectual launchpad in the heart of England.\n\nStepping off the busy hustle of Turl Street into Jesus College reveals a serene world of manicured green lawns and classic Elizabethan architecture. The Fellows' Library is an enchanting time capsule filled with ancient vellum-bound books, while the grand dining hall features a famous portrait of Queen Elizabeth I gazing over students enjoying their evening meals under intricate wooden beams.\n\nYet Jesus College is also one of Oxford’s most innovative modern pioneers. In recent years, it completed the Cheng Yu Tung Building—a state-of-the-art redevelopment combining cutting-edge digital learning hubs with retail spaces and open public access. Add in lively celebrations on St. David’s Day (complete with leek-wearing pride and booming choral anthems), and Jesus College stands out as a brilliant blend of Celtic heritage and 21st-century ambition.","image":"assets/colleges/jesus-college.jpg","lat":51.7538,"lng":-1.257,"founded":1571,"hook":"The Welsh college."},{"id":11,"name":"Keble College","slug":"keble-college","body":"When Keble College opened its doors in 1870, it sent shockwaves through traditional Oxford. While the older colleges were built from understated golden limestone, architect William Butterfield crafted Keble using bold, patterned polychromatic red brick—earning it the affectionate (and sometimes teasing) nickname of the \"streaky bacon\" college. Built as a memorial to church reformer John Keble, it was designed to make an Oxford education accessible to students of modest means.\n\nToday, Keble’s bold design is recognized as a Victorian Gothic masterpiece. Its colossal chapel rivals full-sized cathedrals in scale and holds one of the world's most famous spiritual paintings: *The Light of the World* by Pre-Raphaelite artist William Holman Hunt. Standing inside its towering red-and-gold brick arches, bathed in ethereal light, is an experience that leaves first-time visitors breathless.\n\nOn the ground, Keble is dynamic, sports-obsessed, and immensely proud of its community spirit. It boasts one of the longest dining halls in Oxford—longer even than Christ Church’s—allowing the entire student body to dine together under dramatic vaulted ceilings. With its sleek modern additions like the H B Allen Centre for graduate research, Keble effortlessly blends Victorian daring with high-tech exploration.","image":"assets/colleges/keble-college.jpg","lat":51.759,"lng":-1.2589,"founded":1870,"hook":"Polychrome brick, loudly."},{"id":12,"name":"Kellogg College","slug":"kellogg-college","body":"Kellogg College is Oxford’s vanguard for lifelong learning, flexible study, and international professional development. Founded in 1990 and named in honor of the W.K. Kellogg Foundation, whose generous philanthropic support helped bring it to life, this progressive graduate college was specifically designed to accommodate part-time, executive, and international postgraduate students who balance rigorous academic research with thriving global careers.\n\nLocated in the leafy, Victorian neighborhood of North Oxford along Banbury Road, Kellogg's campus feels like an elegant cluster of welcoming residential villas surrounded by gardens and towering trees. Instead of rigid medieval conventions, the college atmosphere is modern, relaxed, and deeply collaborative, making it an ideal intellectual base for students flying in from Tokyo, Toronto, or Nairobi for intensive research residencies.\n\nKellogg also takes huge pride in sustainability and wellness, boasting Oxford’s first certified Passivhaus building (an ultra-low-energy architectural standard) and an active community garden. Without the stiff hierarchies of older foundations, students and world-class faculty gather at the college bar or over afternoon tea as equal partners in learning, proving that an ancient university can continually reinvent how it educates the world.","image":"assets/colleges/kellogg-college.jpg","lat":51.7604,"lng":-1.2628,"founded":1990,"hook":"The largest college by numbers."},{"id":13,"name":"Lady Margaret Hall","slug":"lady-margaret-hall","body":"Tucked away at the tranquil northern edge of Oxford where the city gives way to University Parks and the River Cherwell, Lady Margaret Hall (LMH) is a sanctuary of radical inclusion. Founded in 1878 as the very first Oxford college for women, it took its name from Lady Margaret Beaufort, a medieval patron of education and mother of King Henry VII. In 1979, having led the historic fight for women's higher education, LMH became fully co-educational.\n\nThe college is famous for its sprawling, park-like grounds, filled with weeping willows, wildflower meadows, and its own private punt house along the river. Designed by legendary architect Sir Giles Gilbert Scott (the genius behind Britain's iconic red telephone boxes), the college's Byzantine-inspired chapel and stately red-brick quads feel like an idyllic country estate hidden right within Oxford's borders.\n\nLMH continues to champion groundbreaking educational access. In 2016, under the leadership of former *Guardian* editor Alan Rusbridger, LMH launched a pioneering Foundation Year program for underrepresented students from disadvantaged backgrounds, a model so successful that the wider university later adopted it campus-wide. With alumni ranging from former Pakistani Prime Minister Benazir Bhutto to Nobel Peace Prize laureate Malala Yousafzai, LMH is a powerhouse of courage and change.","image":"assets/colleges/lady-margaret-hall.jpg","lat":51.7649,"lng":-1.2589,"founded":1878,"hook":"Oxford's first hall for women."},{"id":14,"name":"Linacre College","slug":"linacre-college","body":"Linacre College is a lively, international graduate community that takes its name from Thomas Linacre, the great 15th-century humanist scholar and royal physician who founded the Royal College of Physicians. Established in 1962 to provide a welcoming home for postgraduates from across the globe, Linacre was intentionally designed as an egalitarian society where fellows, staff, and students share every space without traditional academic rank or stuffy barriers.\n\nSituated right beside the lush expanse of University Parks, Linacre’s campus is warm, contemporary, and deeply conscious of the environment. The college is recognized as a leader in campus sustainability, having been one of the first Oxford colleges to achieve carbon-neutral operations and to install solar arrays and green building enhancements across its residential facilities.\n\nWhat truly defines Linacre is its global, multicultural heartbeat. With students representing dozens of nationalities, the dining hall serves up international cuisine, and the common room hosts cultural festivals, lively world music nights, and interdisciplinary research symposiums. It is an intellectual crossroads where environmental scientists, literature scholars, and public health advocates swap ideas late into the evening.","image":"assets/colleges/linacre-college.jpg","lat":51.7589,"lng":-1.2524,"founded":1962,"hook":"Graduates from everywhere."},{"id":15,"name":"Lincoln College","slug":"lincoln-college","body":"If you close your eyes and imagine the quintessential medieval Oxford college, you are probably picturing Lincoln College. Founded in 1427 by Richard Fleming, Bishop of Lincoln, to combat early church heresies, Lincoln has miraculously preserved its intimate 15th-century architecture almost entirely intact. Its golden-limestone quads, creeping ivy, and narrow cobblestone passages look virtually identical to how they appeared over five hundred years ago.\n\nDespite its compact size, Lincoln packs an astonishing cultural and historical punch. Its dining hall is one of the oldest and most atmospheric in Oxford, illuminated entirely by authentic flickering candlelight during formal dinners. Above the front quad looms the college library, housed inside the converted 18th-century All Saints Church, complete with towering Corinthian columns, ornate plaster ceilings, and a stately clocktower that dominates the Oxford skyline.\n\nLincoln is also famous for its warm, down-to-earth student body and quirky folk traditions. Every Ascension Day, the college shares a historic \"Penny Toss\" with neighboring Brasenose, where pennies are thrown from the roof to local schoolchildren while students sip ground-ivy beer. It was also the home of John Wesley, the founder of Methodism, and legendary murder mystery writer John le Carré, lending the college a rich tapestry of faith, philosophy, and espionage lore.","image":"assets/colleges/lincoln-college.jpg","lat":51.7529,"lng":-1.2564,"founded":1427,"hook":"Where Methodism began."},{"id":16,"name":"Magdalen College","slug":"magdalen-college","body":"Pronounced \"Maud-lin,\" this majestic riverside estate is arguably the most breathtakingly romantic college in all of Oxford. Founded in 1458 by William of Waynflete, Magdalen commands vast grounds that include the famous Great Tower, the postcard-perfect Cloisters adorned with bizarre medieval stone gargoyles and hieroglyphs, and Addison’s Walk—a tranquil wooded pathway where C.S. Lewis and J.R.R. Tolkien strolled while debating mythology and faith.\n\nMagdalen even has its very own private deer park, the Grove, where a herd of fallow deer grazes peacefully beneath ancient oak trees just steps away from student dorms. The college’s literary pedigree is second to none: Oscar Wilde held court here as a flamboyant young scholar, filling his rooms with blue china and peacock feathers, while C.S. Lewis spent decades here writing theological essays and dreaming up the snowy world of Narnia.\n\nThe college is also the guardian of Oxford’s most famous spring tradition: May Morning. At 6:00 AM every May 1st, tens of thousands of students and locals gather beneath Magdalen Tower to listen in absolute silence as the college choir sings the Latin hymn *Hymnus Eucharisticus* from the dizzying rooftop. As the final note fades, bells chime, and the entire city erupts into a day of joyous folk dancing and celebration.","image":"assets/colleges/magdalen-college.jpg","lat":51.7521,"lng":-1.247,"founded":1458,"hook":"May Morning from the tower."},{"id":17,"name":"Mansfield College","slug":"mansfield-college","body":"Mansfield College is a shining example of how progressive ideals can transform higher education. Founded in 1886 as a nonconformist theological college—specifically welcoming Protestant dissenters who were historically barred from Oxford due to religious tests—Mansfield moved onto its tranquil site near the University Parks with a clear mission of breaking open closed doors and fostering social justice.\n\nArchitecturally, Mansfield is a hidden gem. Designed by the celebrated Victorian architect Basil Champneys, its central quad is anchored by an awe-inspiring dining hall and chapel lined with ornate wooden carvings, stained glass depicting historic religious and political reformers, and comfortable oak benches. The open green lawn at the front is one of the few quads in Oxford where students are actively encouraged to relax, study, and play without stuffy restrictions.\n\nToday, Mansfield is celebrated as Oxford's leader in social mobility and educational inclusion. Under the recent leadership of Baroness Helena Kennedy, it consistently admits over 90% of its UK undergraduate cohort from state schools, proving that academic excellence and democratic access go hand in hand. It also hosts the prestigious Bonavero Institute of Human Rights, making it a vibrant center for global legal reform and ethical leadership.","image":"assets/colleges/mansfield-college.jpg","lat":51.7565,"lng":-1.2537,"founded":1886,"hook":"Founded for Nonconformists."},{"id":18,"name":"Merton College","slug":"merton-college","body":"Founded in 1264 by Walter de Merton, Lord Chancellor of England, Merton College lays a fierce, well-documented claim to being the oldest true college in Oxford. It was the very first foundation to establish a self-governing community of scholars with a formal set of statutes—a groundbreaking structural model that was later copied by almost every other college in Oxford and Cambridge.\n\nStepping into Merton feels like walking into the bedrock of British history. Its Mob Quad, built in the 14th century, is the oldest surviving quadrangle in the university, and its Upper Library holds the title of the oldest continually operating academic library for scholars in the world, complete with medieval chained books. Merton's 13th-century chapel, with its towering stained-glass rose window, boasts acoustic properties so sublime that sacred choirs travel from around the world to record music within its walls.\n\nMertonians have a well-earned reputation for fierce intellectual discipline (often topping the Norrington Table for Oxford academic exams), but they also celebrate one of Oxford's most delightfully surreal rituals: the Time Ceremony. On the night daylight saving time ends in autumn, students dress in full black-tie formal wear, link arms, and walk backward around the Fellows’ Quad for an hour while drinking port, symbolically \"turning back the cosmic clock\" and keeping the spacetime continuum intact.","image":"assets/colleges/merton-college.jpg","lat":51.7512,"lng":-1.2519,"founded":1264,"hook":"Oldest quad in Oxford."},{"id":19,"name":"New College","slug":"new-college","body":"Do not let the name fool you: New College is anything but new. Founded in 1379 by William of Wykeham, Bishop of Winchester, its full official name is the *College of St Mary of Winchester in Oxford*, but locals called it \"the new college\" to distinguish it from the older St Mary's, and the nickname stuck for over six centuries. It was built on an unprecedented monumental scale, setting the architectural standard for collegiate quads worldwide.\n\nNew College features some of the most dramatic sights in Britain, including a dining hall so majestic it feels like a medieval throne room, an ornate Gothic chapel housing a haunting El Greco masterpiece, and a sweeping garden bordered by the original 13th-century stone city wall of Oxford. Film buffs will instantly recognize its cloistered courtyard and giant ancient oak tree as the filming location where Mad-Eye Moody transformed Malfoy into a ferret in *Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire*.\n\nDespite its ancient grandeur, New College is celebrated for its warm, collaborative vibe and world-class musical traditions. The New College Choir is internationally celebrated, filling the quads with angelic polyphony several evenings a week. Add in a friendly rivalry with Winchester College and a passionate tradition of student theatrical productions on the garden mound, and New College remains a pinnacle of Oxford culture.","image":"assets/colleges/new-college.jpg","lat":51.7543,"lng":-1.2513,"founded":1379,"hook":"New since 1379."},{"id":20,"name":"Nuffield College","slug":"nuffield-college","body":"Nuffield College stands apart as a sleek, specialized powerhouse dedicated strictly to postgraduate research in the social sciences, economics, politics, and sociology. Founded in 1937 through the visionary philanthropy of William Morris, Lord Nuffield—the legendary industrialist behind Morris Motors—the college was created to build a cooperative bridge between the academic world and practical public policymakers.\n\nIts architecture is instantly recognizable and totally unique in Oxford. Designed by Austen Harrison in a refined \"Cotswold vernacular\" style, Nuffield combines traditional honey-colored limestone with sleek mid-century Art Deco lines. Its towering central spire is actually not a church steeple, but a multi-story library tower designed specifically to hold vast archives of political data, international policy documents, and economic manuscripts.\n\nNuffield’s academic culture is intense, collaborative, and deeply influential on real-world governance. Cabinet ministers, central bankers, and leading social scientists regularly pass through its seminar rooms to test economic models and debate legislative policy. Because every student is a fully funded or specialized researcher, the social atmosphere feels like a high-level think tank where casual hallway chats can end up shaping government policy.","image":"assets/colleges/nuffield-college.jpg","lat":51.7527,"lng":-1.2634,"founded":1937,"hook":"Social science, built by a carmaker."},{"id":21,"name":"Oriel College","slug":"oriel-college","body":"Founded in 1326 by King Edward II and his almoner Adam de Brome, Oriel College holds the proud title of the oldest royal foundation in Oxford. Its nickname derives from \"La Oriole,\" a grand medieval property that stood on the original site. Nestled right against the cobblestones of Oriel Square, the college is a dazzling display of 17th-century Jacobean architecture, marked by ornate Flemish gables and an iconic grand stone staircase leading up to its dining hall.\n\nIn the 19th century, Oriel became the epicenter of a seismic religious and philosophical revolution known as the Oxford Movement. Brilliant thinkers like John Henry Newman, John Keble, and Edward Pusey gathered in Oriel's senior common room, publishing radical tracts that sought to restore historic Catholic traditions and liturgical beauty to the Church of England, shaking Victorian intellectual life to its core.\n\nOn the lighter side of history, Oriel is legendary on the water. It is historically one of the most dominant rowing colleges in Oxford, frequently winning the prestigious \"Head of the River\" title in both the Torpids and Summer Eights bumps races. The college celebrates these rowing victories by joyously burning a wooden rowing boat in the center of the front quad—a fiery, high-spirited tradition that echoes centuries of athletic rivalry.","image":"assets/colleges/oriel-college.jpg","lat":51.7518,"lng":-1.2539,"founded":1326,"hook":"The oldest royal foundation."},{"id":22,"name":"Pembroke College","slug":"pembroke-college","body":"Pembroke College, founded in 1624 during the reign of King James I and named after William Herbert, 3rd Earl of Pembroke, is a warm and welcoming academic haven tucked just across the street from Christ Church. While smaller in scale than its gargantuan neighbor, Pembroke boasts a fiercely loyal student body, a delightfully vibrant social culture, and a history packed with literary and political giants.\n\nThe college’s most celebrated son is none other than Samuel Johnson, the legendary 18th-century lexicographer who single-handedly wrote the definitive *Dictionary of the English Language*. Johnson's teapot, writing desk, and portrait are lovingly preserved in the college, and his legacy of witty conversation and relentless curiosity still permeates Pembroke's bustling common rooms and scenic, flower-filled quads.\n\nIn recent decades, Pembroke has grown dramatically with the addition of the sleek Rokos Quad, connected to the historic campus via a graceful pedestrian footbridge over Brewer Street. The college is also renowned for its passionate commitment to student welfare, a powerhouse rowing club, and a famously delicious student dining service. At Pembroke, you get the full weight of Oxford heritage without an ounce of pretension.","image":"assets/colleges/pembroke-college.jpg","lat":51.7501,"lng":-1.2578,"founded":1624,"hook":"Dr Johnson's college."},{"id":23,"name":"The Queen's College","slug":"the-queen-s-college","body":"Walking down Oxford’s iconic High Street, it is impossible to miss the jaw-dropping, sunlit frontage of The Queen's College. Founded in 1341 by Robert de Eglesfield, chaplain to Queen Philippa of Hainault, the college was completely rebuilt in the late 17th and early 18th centuries in a breathtaking neoclassical Roman Baroque style, making it look more like a magnificent Italian palazzo than a chilly English school.\n\nThe college’s Upper Library is widely regarded as one of the most beautiful rooms in Europe, boasting soaring stucco ceilings crafted by master plasterer Thomas Roberts, towering arched windows, and priceless collections of early scientific and classical works. The Front Quad, framed by magnificent covered Doric colonnades and Nicholas Hawksmoor’s stately cupola, creates an acoustic amphitheater that resonates with absolute grandeur.\n\nQueen's is also home to some of Oxford’s most eccentric medieval culinary ceremonies. Every Christmas, the college hosts the Boar's Head Feast, where a decorated wild boar's head is carried into the dining hall on a silver platter accompanied by a trumpet fanfare and the singing of a 15th-century carol. On New Year’s Day, the Bursar presents every dining guest with a needle and thread, whispering the French pun *\"Prenez ceci et soyez dépensier\"* (\"Be thrifty\"), playing on the founder’s name Eglesfield (*aiguille et fil*).","image":"assets/colleges/the-queen-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7532,"lng":-1.251,"founded":1341,"hook":"Summoned to dinner by trumpet."},{"id":24,"name":"Reuben College","slug":"reuben-college","body":"Reuben College is the newest constituent star in the Oxford constellation. Established in 2019 (initially as Parks College) and renamed in 2020 following a transformative landmark gift from the Reuben Foundation, this innovative graduate college was engineered from the ground up to tackle the defining challenges of the 21st century: Artificial Intelligence, environmental change, cellular life, and ethics.\n\nRather than building an isolated campus on the edge of the city, Reuben took up residence right in the beating heart of Oxford's historic Science Area. The college has brilliantly revitalized the historic Worthington Building and the old Radcliffe Science Library, transforming these heritage spaces into a high-tech paradise featuring collaborative labs, digital research clusters, and light-filled study zones.\n\nReuben rejects traditional academic silos in favor of interdisciplinary collisions. Here, machine learning engineers dine alongside bioethicists, and climate scientists brainstorm climate mitigation with legal scholars. With its ultra-modern commitment to sustainability, public engagement, and diverse global perspectives, Reuben represents the exciting frontier of where Oxford is headed over the next century.","image":"assets/colleges/reuben-college.jpg","lat":51.758,"lng":-1.2555,"founded":2019,"hook":"Oxford's newest college."},{"id":25,"name":"St Anne's College","slug":"st-anne-s-college","body":"St Anne’s College began its journey in 1879 as the Association for the Education of Women, providing a vital pathway for female students who lived in private lodgings around Oxford and were denied membership in the existing all-male colleges. Earning full collegiate status by royal charter in 1952 and going co-ed in 1979, St Anne's has remained fiercely committed to its founding DNA: independence, progressive values, and radical openness.\n\nSituated on a spacious, leafy green campus between Woodstock and Banbury Roads, St Anne’s offers a relaxed, modern environment free from the rigid tourist crowds of the city center. Its architectural crown jewel is the Tim Gardam Building, a stunning modern library and humanities center complete with rooftop reading terraces, soundproof study pods, and a vibrant student café that serves as the social heartbeat of the college.\n\nSt Anne’s pride itself on a down-to-earth, unpretentious atmosphere where students are encouraged to be boldly themselves. It holds one of the largest and most well-equipped college libraries in Oxford, open 24/7, making it a beloved sanctuary for night-owl scholars. With notable alumni like legendary author Iris Murdoch and former UK High Court judges, St Anne's proves that you do not need medieval gargoyles to cultivate world-changing brilliance.","image":"assets/colleges/st-anne-s-college.jpg","lat":51.762,"lng":-1.2648,"founded":1878,"hook":"Began as a home-study society."},{"id":26,"name":"St Antony's College","slug":"st-antony-s-college","body":"If the United Nations decided to establish a graduate research college in the heart of England, it would look exactly like St Antony’s. Founded in 1950 through the generosity of French shipping magnate and philanthropist Antonin Besse, St Antony’s specializes exclusively in international relations, regional studies, global economics, and modern diplomatic history.\n\nThe college’s architectural landscape is an exhilarating dialogue between Victorian heritage and world-class modern design. Alongside handsome 19th-century brick mansions stands the dramatic Investcorp Building, a gleaming, futuristic stainless-steel swoop designed by visionary architect Dame Zaha Hadid. The building curves gracefully around historic trees, housing the Middle East Centre’s archives, seminar rooms, and an underground auditorium.\n\nWith students representing over 70 different nations at any given moment, St Antony’s boasts one of the most cosmopolitan student bodies in the world. Walk into the dining hall on any given afternoon, and you will hear foreign policy debates unfolding across a dozen languages. It is a legendary training ground for diplomats, foreign correspondents, ambassadors, and NGO leaders who go on to shape international politics.","image":"assets/colleges/st-antony-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7625,"lng":-1.2628,"founded":1950,"hook":"International affairs."},{"id":27,"name":"St Catherine's College","slug":"st-catherine-s-college","body":"Affectionately known to everyone in Oxford as \"Catz,\" St Catherine's College is a breathtaking, unapologetic triumph of 20th-century modernist architecture. Founded in 1962 by the visionary historian Alan Bullock, the entire college was masterminded by the celebrated Danish architect Arne Jacobsen, who designed not just the buildings, but every single detail—from the brick walls and reflection pools down to the famous minimalist chairs, door handles, and custom cutlery.\n\nSet among peaceful water meadows near the River Cherwell, Catz feels like a serene Scandinavian retreat. Jacobsen's design integrates glass, bronze, concrete, and brick with manicured hedges and weeping willows, creating an open, egalitarian layout where interior study rooms blend seamlessly with tranquil outdoor courtyards. It holds the distinction of being recognized as a Grade I listed architectural masterwork.\n\nCulturally, Catz is renowned as one of the largest, friendliest, and most socially active colleges in Oxford. Because the college was built on principles of modern equality, it has no traditional raised High Table in its dining hall, symbolizing that students and professors share a common pursuit of knowledge. With its thriving music scene, vibrant drama society, and famously welcoming bar, Catz is a paradise for free spirits.","image":"assets/colleges/st-catherine-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7563,"lng":-1.244,"founded":1962,"hook":"Modernism in a medieval city."},{"id":28,"name":"St Cross College","slug":"st-cross-college","body":"Tucked discreetly behind St Giles' on Pusey Street, St Cross College is one of Oxford’s best-kept secrets. Founded in 1965 to provide an academic home for postgraduates and university fellows, St Cross was built on the core values of egalitarian community, global friendship, and cross-disciplinary collaboration. \n\nThe college is centered around the serene Richard Blackwell Quad and the historic Pusey House site, blending handsome Gothic Revival stonework with sleek, eco-friendly modern additions like the West Wing. The central quad garden, with its peaceful fountain and blooming flowerbeds, provides a quiet sanctuary where researchers can step away from the laboratory or archives to decompress under open skies.\n\nWhat makes St Cross truly unique is its warm, non-hierarchical culture. In the tradition of Oxford’s newer graduate societies, there is no separate dining room or high table for faculty; master scholars, postdocs, and first-year masters students eat lunch side by side at long wooden tables. It is an unpretentious, deeply supportive environment where international researchers find a true home away from home.","image":"assets/colleges/st-cross-college.jpg","lat":51.7565,"lng":-1.2549,"founded":1965,"hook":"Small, graduate, quietly excellent."},{"id":29,"name":"St Edmund Hall","slug":"st-edmund-hall","body":"Known universally across Oxford simply as \"Teddy Hall,\" St Edmund Hall holds a cherished, colorful identity as the oldest surviving academic society dedicated to undergraduate education. Tracing its roots all the way back to the 13th century when St Edmund of Abingdon taught scholars on this very spot, it is the sole surviving medieval academic \"hall\" in Oxford, having achieved full college status by royal charter in 1957.\n\nEntering Teddy Hall feels like stepping into a cozy medieval wonderland. The tiny, picturesque Front Quad is home to a historic 12th-century stone well inscribed with Latin script, while the college library is housed inside the converted 12th-century Norman church of St Peter-in-the-East, complete with ancient tombstones and a peaceful medieval graveyard garden where students read under the shade of ancient yew trees.\n\nBeyond its ancient charm, Teddy Hall is legendary for possessing what is widely regarded as the most spirited, close-knit, and passionate college community in the university. Its students are famously enthusiastic about sports, dominating rugby and rowing competitions, while also running an incredible creative writing and theatrical scene. The phrase \"Hall Spirit\" is famous across Oxford—and one evening in the Teddy Hall buttery will show you exactly why.","image":"assets/colleges/st-edmund-hall.jpg","lat":51.7527,"lng":-1.2503,"founded":1236,"hook":"The last medieval hall."},{"id":30,"name":"St Hilda's College","slug":"st-hilda-s-college","body":"Perched gracefully on the east bank of the River Cherwell beside Magdalen Bridge, St Hilda’s College enjoys what is arguably the most picturesque waterside location in the entire university. Founded in 1893 by pioneering educationist Dorothea Beale as a hall for women, St Hilda’s was the last remaining all-female college in Oxford, proudly carrying that trailblazing feminist legacy until it voted to become fully co-educational in 2008.\n\nThe college’s expansive riverside gardens are an idyllic paradise where weeping willows drape over the water, students study on rolling green lawns, and the college’s private fleet of wooden punts is moored right at the garden steps. Its modern Anniversary Building, completed in recent years, features a striking curved brick pavilion and a tower that offers panoramic rooftop views across the Oxford skyline and Christ Church Meadow.\n\nSt Hilda’s has an exceptionally rich musical, artistic, and literary heritage. It is home to the Jacqueline du Pré Music Building, a world-class acoustic concert auditorium named in honor of the legendary cellist, hosting international chamber concerts and student recitals throughout the year. The vibe here is relaxed, creative, and down-to-earth—an ideal sanctuary for students who want riverside serenity just a short stroll from the city center.","image":"assets/colleges/st-hilda-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7472,"lng":-1.2455,"founded":1893,"hook":"On the Cherwell's bend."},{"id":31,"name":"St Hugh's College","slug":"st-hugh-s-college","body":"Spread across fourteen glorious acres of lush private gardens in North Oxford, St Hugh’s College is often called \"the island\" of tranquility. Founded in 1886 by Elizabeth Wordsworth (great-niece of the famous romantic poet William Wordsworth) to support women of limited financial means, the college was named after St Hugh of Lincoln. It transitioned into a co-educational college in 1986, retaining its founding spirit of social mobility and independent thinking.\n\nSt Hugh's feels like a grand Edwardian country manor surrounded by woodland walks, sprawling manicured lawns, and vibrant flower borders. Its magnificent Mordan Hall and Main Building are complemented by the Dickson Poon University of Oxford China Centre Building, a state-of-the-art hub complete with traditional Chinese-inspired courtyards, a dedicated library, and cutting-edge lecture theaters.\n\nThe student experience at St Hugh’s is defined by space, community, and peace of mind. While city-center colleges can feel cramped and overrun with tourists, St Hugh's students enjoy enormous green lawns where they can play croquet, read under giant beech trees, or host summer barbecues. Notable alumni include international leaders like former UK Prime Minister Theresa May and Nobel laureate Aung San Suu Kyi.","image":"assets/colleges/st-hugh-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7669,"lng":-1.2649,"founded":1886,"hook":"Fourteen acres of garden."},{"id":32,"name":"St John's College","slug":"st-john-s-college","body":"St John’s College is an absolute titan: historically, architecturally, and financially. Founded in 1555 by wealthy London merchant Sir Thomas White to provide educated clergy for the Counter-Reformation, St John’s grew over the centuries into the wealthiest college in Oxford. This immense endowment allows the college to provide world-class student facilities, generous research grants, and heavily subsidized student living.\n\nThe college grounds are a spectacular journey through English architectural history. The Canterbury Quad, completed in 1636 under Archbishop William Laud, is an Italianate Renaissance masterpiece featuring bronze statues of King Charles I and Queen Henrietta Maria. Beyond the stone quads lie five acres of the most immaculate, award-winning botanical gardens in Oxford, filled with rare specimen trees, rockeries, and sweeping lawns that look like paintings.\n\nSt John’s boasts an intellectual and cultural roster that spans centuries, counting writers like Robert Graves and A.E. Housman, as well as political figures like former Prime Minister Tony Blair, among its alumni. Despite its immense wealth and ancient prestige, St John's fosters a vibrant, welcoming student body that excels across arts, sports, and academics, consistently ranking near the top of university league tables.","image":"assets/colleges/st-john-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7573,"lng":-1.26,"founded":1555,"hook":"The wealthiest college."},{"id":33,"name":"St Peter's College","slug":"st-peter-s-college","body":"Standing on the historic site of medieval hostels in central Oxford, St Peter’s College is a vibrant, friendly, and progressive academic community. Founded in 1929 by Francis James Chavasse, former Bishop of Liverpool, the college was designed with the noble goal of making an Oxford education affordable and accessible to promising scholars from diverse, working-class backgrounds, achieving full collegiate status in 1961.\n\nThe college’s charming campus is an architectural mosaic blending historic Georgian townhouses, an elegant 19th-century church building (now the college chapel), and award-winning contemporary structures. Its cozy quads are framed by the former Canal House and Linton House, creating an intimate, village-like atmosphere located just steps away from Oxford’s bustling shopping streets and cultural landmarks.\n\nSt Peter’s is renowned university-wide for its remarkably friendly, inclusive social atmosphere and a legendary music and drama scene. The college chapel boasts incredible acoustics and an acclaimed student choir, while the student-run bar—affectionately built inside a historic former brewery cellar—is one of the liveliest social hubs in town. At St Peter’s, warmth, community, and creative energy always take center stage.","image":"assets/colleges/st-peter-s-college.jpg","lat":51.7527,"lng":-1.2601,"founded":1929,"hook":"Built from a parish church."},{"id":34,"name":"Somerville College","slug":"somerville-college","body":"Named in honor of the brilliant 19th-century mathematician, astronomer, and polymath Mary Somerville, this storied institution was founded in 1879 as a non-sectarian hall for women. Unlike many colleges of its era, Somerville refused to impose religious tests on its applicants, fostering an atmosphere of fierce intellectual independence, progressive debate, and radical questioning from its very first day.\n\nThe college campus is a peaceful green oasis located on Woodstock Road, featuring the grand Victorian Maitland Building and the sprawling Catherine Hughes Building. Somerville’s library is legendary: with over 120,000 volumes, it is one of the largest undergraduate libraries in Oxford, built to ensure that female scholars had access to every possible book at a time when they were barred from certain university collections.\n\nSomerville’s roll call of visionary alumni is truly awe-inspiring. It produced Britain’s first female prime minister, Margaret Thatcher, as well as India’s first female prime minister, Indira Gandhi. It was also the intellectual cradle for Nobel Prize-winning chemist Dorothy Hodgkin, who solved the atomic structures of penicillin and insulin. Today, as a co-educational college, Somerville continues to nurture brilliant minds who question orthodoxy and reshape the world.","image":"assets/colleges/somerville-college.jpg","lat":51.7594,"lng":-1.2637,"founded":1879,"hook":"Thatcher, Hodgkin, Sayers."},{"id":35,"name":"Trinity College","slug":"trinity-college","body":"Founded in 1555 by Sir Thomas Pope on the grounds of an older monastic house, Trinity College is a breathtaking vision of classical elegance, sprawling lawns, and peerless landscaping. Pope purchased the site of the former Durham College following the Dissolution of the Monasteries, creating an academic sanctuary dedicated to Catholic-leaning humanist scholarship that later flourished into one of Oxford’s grandest foundations.\n\nTrinity's grounds are famous for their sheer beauty and spaciousness. The college chapel, designed with input from Sir Christopher Wren, features an exquisite carved cedarwood altarpiece crafted by the legendary Grinling Gibbons, filling the air with a faint, centuries-old fragrance of cedar. Beyond the stately Durham and Garden Quads lies Trinity's magnificent lawn and famous Lime Walk, where neatly pleached lime trees form a romantic green tunnel.\n\nTrinity shares one of the most famous, good-natured student rivalries in Oxford with its immediate neighbor, Balliol College. For hundreds of years, students have engaged in witty pranks and chants over the dividing wall. With alumni ranging from Cardinal John Henry Newman and three British Prime Ministers (including William Pitt the Elder) to modern comedic icons, Trinity combines aristocratic grandeur with high-spirited student life.","image":"assets/colleges/trinity-college.jpg","lat":51.7551,"lng":-1.2568,"founded":1555,"hook":"Balliol's ancient rival."},{"id":36,"name":"University College","slug":"university-college","body":"Known across Oxford simply as \"Univ,\" University College claims the legendary title of being the oldest college of them all. While a fanciful medieval myth claimed that King Alfred the Great founded the college in the year 872, the actual historical foundation dates to 1249 through a bequest from William of Durham. Whether you believe the myth or the documents, Univ stands at the very dawn of collegiate university history.\n\nOccupying a prominent curved frontage along the historic High Street, Univ’s sweeping stone quads exude timeless majesty. In a quiet, domed chamber off the front quad lies the Shelley Memorial—a stunning, poignant white marble statue depicting the romantic poet Percy Bysshe Shelley, who was notoriously expelled from Univ in 1811 for co-authoring a pamphlet defending atheism, only for the college to later celebrate him as one of its greatest literary sons.\n\nUniv is also famous for its warmth, academic excellence, and world-shaping alumni, including U.S. President Bill Clinton, British Prime Minister Clement Attlee, and scientific visionary Stephen Hawking, who studied physics here as an undergraduate. With its lively college choir, active sports teams, and the beloved tradition of holding an annual banquet celebrating King Alfred, Univ wears its colossal history with infectious energy.","image":"assets/colleges/university-college.jpg","lat":51.7521,"lng":-1.2523,"founded":1249,"hook":"Claims King Alfred founded it."},{"id":37,"name":"Wadham College","slug":"wadham-college","body":"Founded in 1610 by Nicholas and Dorothy Wadham, this college holds a legendary place in the history of human thought and social liberty. Following her husband’s death, Dorothy Wadham took full charge of the project from her home in Somerset, becoming the first woman who was not a royal or a nun to found an Oxford college, personally drafting its statutes and directing its construction with incredible precision.\n\nIn the mid-17th century, Wadham became the birthplace of the scientific revolution in England. The visionary Warden John Wilkins hosted a circle of brilliant natural philosophers—including architect Christopher Wren, chemist Robert Boyle, and polymath Robert Hooke—in his lodgings at Wadham. Their experimental meetings directly gave birth to the Royal Society, transforming modern scientific inquiry forever.\n\nToday, Wadham is internationally celebrated as Oxford’s most progressive, activist, and radically welcoming community. The college is a proud pioneer of LGBTQ+ inclusion, social equality, and student-led democracy. Its tranquil Fellows' Garden, framed by ancient trees and modern sculptures, hosts the famous Queerfest and Wadstock music festivals, proving that a college can honor a 400-year-old scientific legacy while remaining the cutting-edge cultural vanguard of student life.","image":"assets/colleges/wadham-college.jpg","lat":51.756,"lng":-1.2549,"founded":1610,"hook":"Cradle of the Royal Society."},{"id":38,"name":"Wolfson College","slug":"wolfson-college","body":"Perched serenely along the gentle banks of the River Cherwell in North Oxford, Wolfson College is a dynamic, egalitarian graduate paradise. Founded in 1966 and named after industrialist and philanthropist Sir Isaac Wolfson, the college's founding president was the towering philosopher and political theorist Sir Isaiah Berlin, who envisioned a modern collegiate community completely free from stuffy medieval snobbery and archaic hierarchies.\n\nThe college’s architecture, designed by Powell & Moya, is a Grade II listed modernist masterwork that embraces its riverside landscape. Its stepped concrete terraces, open footbridges, and glass-walled common rooms look directly over the water, where resident swans glide past and students untie college punts from the private harbor for lazy afternoon journeys down the river into the countryside.\n\nTrue to Isaiah Berlin's founding vision, Wolfson operates on strict principles of democratic equality: there is no High Table, dress codes are relaxed, and professors, graduate researchers, and administrative staff share the exact same dining hall and common rooms. With a thriving international community, dedicated family housing, and its own resident herd of wild geese, Wolfson is a refreshingly modern, idyllic haven for advanced research.","image":"assets/colleges/wolfson-college.jpg","lat":51.7692,"lng":-1.2568,"founded":1966,"hook":"Its own harbour on the Cherwell."},{"id":39,"name":"Worcester College","slug":"worcester-college","body":"Fondly nicknamed the \"country estate in the city,\" Worcester College offers what many consider the most enchanting and varied landscape in all of Oxford. Founded in 1714 through the benefaction of Sir Thomas Cookes (incorporating the surviving 13th-century buildings of Gloucester College, an ancient Benedictine monastery), Worcester is a magical architectural collision where medieval monks' cottages sit directly opposite an imposing 18th-century neoclassical terrace.\n\nBeyond its grand stone facades lies Worcester’s true wonderland: twenty-six acres of pristine botanical gardens, rolling sports fields, a tranquil orchard, and a sparkling private lake bordered by weeping willows and water lilies. It is the only central Oxford college that has its athletic playing fields located directly inside the main college grounds, allowing students to step straight from the library onto the cricket pitch.\n\nThe interior of Worcester's chapel is another mind-blowing spectacle, lavishly redesigned by Victorian architect William Burges into a psychedelic jewel box of gilded animals, mosaic floors, and vibrant frescos. Combine that artistic beauty with a famously warm student body, a lively theater society, and ducks wandering happily across the lawns, and Worcester delivers an unforgettable finale to the 39 colleges of Oxford.","image":"assets/colleges/worcester-college.jpg","lat":51.7546,"lng":-1.2637,"founded":1714,"hook":"A lake, and a black swan."}];
